 ### Parameters
-*   `xdiv`, `ydiv`, `zdiv`: Number of divisions along X, Y, Z axes. `P = xdiv * ydiv * zdiv` [S1, S2, S3, unofficial].
-*   `step_size`: Ray marching step size (e.g., 0.5) [S1, S3, S4, unofficial].
-*   `input_dataset`: Path to `.raw` file [S1, unofficial].
-*   `color_tf`, `opacity_tf`: Paths to TF files [S1, unofficial].
+*   `xdiv, ydiv, zdiv`: Number of divisions along each axis. Must satisfy $xdiv \cdot ydiv \cdot zdiv = P$ [S2, S3, unofficial].
+*   `step_size`: Ray marching step size. Typical values: $0.5$ to $2.0$ depending on resolution [S3, S4, unofficial].
+*   `dataset_filename`: Path to `.raw` file [S1, unofficial].
+*   `color_tf`, `opacity_tf`: Paths to text files [S1, unofficial].
 
 ### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Boundary Calculation:** The remainder `X % xdiv` must be distributed to the first blocks. If `rem` is not decremented correctly, block sizes will be incorrect [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Division by Zero:** Transfer function interpolation must check if `v1 == v0` to avoid division by zero [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Index Out of Bounds:** When interpolating Z values, `z1` must be clamped to `data.shape[2] - 1` [S4, unofficial].
-*   **Data Order:** Volume data must be loaded and reshaped in the correct order (Fortran order is specified in some implementations) [S1, S3, S4, unofficial].
+*   **Boundary Calculation:** The remainder distribution logic (`rem--`) must be implemented carefully to ensure block sizes are correct and sum to the total dimension [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Division by Zero:** Transfer function interpolation must check if $v_1 == v_0$ to avoid division by zero [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Index Out of Bounds:** When interpolating Z-values, $z_1$ must be clamped to $Z_{loc} - 1$ [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Load Imbalance:** Fixed 3D decomposition may lead to load imbalance if the volume's opacity distribution is non-uniform, as some processes may cast rays through dense regions while others cast through empty space [S1, S4, unofficial].
+*   **Communication Overhead:** Distributing large volumes and gathering images can be a bottleneck. Using `MPI_Scatterv` and `MPI_Gatherv` is more efficient than `MPI_Send`/`MPI_Recv` loops for unequal block sizes [S2, S4, unofficial].
