 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(P \cdot \frac{V}{P} \cdot R)$, where $P$ is the number of processes, $V$ is the volume size, and $R$ is the number of rays per process. Communication overhead is $O(V)$ for data distribution and $O(I)$ for image gathering, where $I$ is the image size [S1, S4, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(V/P)$ per process for the local volume chunk [S2, S4, unofficial].
-*   **Scaling:** Scales well with the number of processes if the volume is large enough to balance the communication overhead [S1, S4, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Distributed memory parallelism allows rendering of very large datasets that do not fit in a single node's memory [S1, S4, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Communication overhead for data distribution and image gathering can become significant for small datasets or high process counts [S1, S4, unofficial]. Load imbalance can occur if the volume density is uneven [S4, unofficial].
+
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(P \cdot \frac{X \cdot Y \cdot Z}{P} \cdot R)$, where $R$ is the average number of samples per ray. Communication overhead is $O(X \cdot Y)$ for gathering images [S1, S4, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(\frac{X \cdot Y \cdot Z}{P})$ per process for the local volume subdomain [S2, S4, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Scales well with the number of processes if the volume is large enough to keep all processes busy [S1, unofficial]. Performance is limited by network bandwidth during data distribution and image gathering [S2, S4, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Scalable to large datasets and clusters; simple decomposition logic; leverages distributed memory [S1, S4, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Load imbalance if opacity is non-uniform; communication overhead for large images; fixed decomposition may not match data aspect ratio optimally [S1, S4, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** Large volumetric datasets that fit across multiple nodes; uniform opacity distribution; fixed view direction [S1, S4, unofficial].
+*   **When Not to Use:** Small datasets that fit on a single node; highly non-uniform opacity distribution without load balancing; interactive rendering requiring low latency [S1, S4, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-This method is implemented in various open-source projects such as `Parallel_Distribution_Axis-Aligned_Volume_Rendering` and `Parallel-3D-Volume-Rendering-with-MPI` [S1, S4, unofficial]. It builds on the principles of MPI-based parallel computing and volume ray casting [S1, S4, unofficial].
+
+MPI Volume Rendering (3D Decomposition) is a standard approach in high-performance scientific visualization, leveraging the Message Passing Interface (MPI) for distributed memory parallelism [S1, S4, unofficial]. It is commonly used in projects like `Parallel_Distribution_Axis-Aligned_Volume_Rendering` and `Parallel-3D-Volume-Rendering-with-MPI` [S1, S4, unofficial]. The method builds on the principles of distributed ray casting, where the volume is partitioned spatially to allow parallel processing [S2, S3, unofficial]. It is an evolution of 1D and 2D decomposition methods, offering more flexibility in matching the process grid to the data dimensions [S1, S3, unofficial].
 
